    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `customerCode` is computed in `AssignCustomerCode` (step `assignCustomerCode` in `owners.POST.yml`). The base code is `<REGION>-<HASH8>`. I added collision de-duplication:\n\n- Injected `OwnerRepository` into the step (same by-type injection the sibling `Assign*` steps use).\n- Before saving, it collects all existing owners' `customerCode`s (via `findAll()`, which \u2014 running after `BuildOwner` and before `SaveOwner` \u2014 returns only pre-existing owners, matching the pattern in `AssignNamesakeCount`).\n- If the base code is free, it's used as-is. Otherwise it appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2` and incrementing until it finds a code not already in use, then sets that.\n\n```java\nString customerCode = baseCode;\nfor (int n = 2; existingCodes.contains(customerCode); n++) {\n    customerCode = baseCode + \"-\" + n;\n}\nowner.setCustomerCode(customerCode);\n```\n\nThis runs before `AssignMembershipNumber`, which derives from `customerCode`, so the de-duplicated value flows through correctly. The entity is mutated in place per the pipeline's `Out`/`@Val` convention.",
